WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.199 --> 00:00:09.130
Scientists calculated that Santa would have
to visit 822 homes a second to deliver all

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:15.799
the world's presents on Christmas Eve, travelling
at 650 miles a second.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:19.670
The word Xmas isn't actually blasphemous.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.410
The letter X is a Greek abbreviation for Christ.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.140
And guess what?

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:33.800
It's likely that Jesus wasn't born on December
25th, 1AD, many theologians estimate he was

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.570
born sometime in September between 2BC and
7BC.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.820
Which means the calendar as we know it may
be out by a few years.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.440
Furthermore it's believed Jesus was actually
born in a cave, not a stable.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.500
So why do we celebrate Christmas on the 25th
of December?

00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:00.420
There are many theories to this, a popular
theory is that December 25th was chosen because

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.290
the Winter Solstice and the ancient pagan
Roman midwinter festivals called 'Saturnalia'

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:12.139
and 'Dies Natalis Solis Invicti' took place
in December around this date.

00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:23.670
So it was more convenient to have all festivities
at the same time.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:30.090
There are some unusual Christmas traditions
around the world, for example, in Iceland

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:37.149
there are 13 different Santas including one
called "Grýla", who is a horrifying old woman

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:41.689
who kidnaps children on Christmas if they've
been naughty.

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:48.789
In India fir trees and pine trees are extremely
scarce because of the climate, so Indians

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:53.219
decorate banana trees and mango trees instead.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:02:02.549
In 1966 a 13-metre tall straw goat was erected
in the town square of Galve in Sweden.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:08.630
At the stroke of midnight on Christmas Eve
a vandal burned down the goat.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:16.569
But this didn't dissuade the town, every year
since the town has re-erected the goat and

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:20.400
every year vandals attempt to burn it down.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.849
The goat has been burned down more than 25
times.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:32.689
In fact, it has happened so often that in
1988 bookmakers began taking bets on the goats

00:02:32.689 --> 00:02:33.689
survival.

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:39.709
And in case you were wondering, no the town
doesn't want the goat to be burnt down, in

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:45.750
fact, in 2001 an American tourist served jail
time for successfully doing so.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.969
But you ain't heard nothing yet.

00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:54.120
The prize for the most uncanny Christmas traditions
goes to, you guessed it, Japan.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.110
The Japanese are obsessed with KFC on Christmas
day.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:04.900
On Christmas day people queue outside KFC's
all around the country to buy their Christmas

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.780
buckets, people even order months in advance
to avoid the queues.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:16.030
This strange tradition is a result of a massive
multi-year marketing campaign in Japan by

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:22.329
KFC to associate Christmas with KFC and it
worked.

00:03:22.329 --> 00:03:27.950
Christmas sponge cakes, with whipped cream,
strawberries and chocolate on top are also

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.990
a popular Christmas day custom in Japan.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:38.390
KFC picked up on this growing tradition and
now offer a Christmas day menu in Japan, including

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:44.920
a bucket of fried chicken that has a cardboard
bottom with a Christmas cake underneath and

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.830
a bottle of champagne.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.560
There are countless variations of Santa Claus
all around the world, namely St. Nicholas,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.519
Father Christmas and Santa Claus.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:58.000
But what's the difference between them?

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.349
They all originated from one man, Saint Nicholas.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:10.230
St. Nicholas was a Bishop who lived in Myra
in Asia Minor, which is now called Turkey,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.780
around the fourth century AD.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.830
He was a very wealthy because his parents
died when he was young and left him a lot

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:18.940
of money.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.880
He was also very kind and had a reputation
for helping the poor.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:29.630
There are several legends surrounding St.
Nicholas, one famous story tells us how one

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:36.940
night St. Nicholas secretly dropped a bag
of gold down the chimney of a poor family.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:42.780
The bag fell into a stocking that had been
hung by the fire to dry.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.880
This is believed to be where the custom of
hanging up christmas stockings originated

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:47.880
from.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.580
By the 16th century the stories about St.
Nicholas had become very unpopular, but someone

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.570
had to deliver presents to children at Christmas
so various countries invented their own mysterious

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:00.860
gift givers.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:07.300
The UK came up with Father Christmas, in France
he was known as Père Nöel.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.150
Germany named him 'Christ Kind'.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.160
In the early USA he was named [IMAGE]' Kris
Kringle'.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.530
However later on, Dutch settlers in the USA
took the old stories of St. Nicholas with

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:25.740
them and came up with a new name for him 'Sinterklaas'
which turned into the now, widely adopted,

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:26.740
Santa Claus.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:28.880
But where does he actually live?

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.770
There's a lot of debate over this, The UK,
USA and Cananda think he lives in the North

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:42.370
Pole, the Norwegians believe he's in Drøbak,
the Danes say he's actually in Greenland.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:49.440
But the Finns are adamant he's in Joulukka,
Lapland and Finland's tourist board are out

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.180
to prove it.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:59.250
Santa's Lapland property is an impressive
86-acres, he has his very own managing director.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:06.210
Private meetings can be arranged with Santa
for a hefty $1,360.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:12.750
Each year more than 70,000 visitors come to
Joulukka to visit Santa and the post office

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:18.190
receives more than 500,000 letters from children
around the globe.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:25.140
Santa is able to reply to about 40,000 of
them in up to 12 different languages.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.960
So does Santa really live in Lapland?

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.002
Well, at the end of the day, almost every
country has its own traditions and stories

00:06:32.002 --> 00:06:36.680
surrounding Santa Claus, so it's really up
to you who you want to believe.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:42.040
Now for the big question, did Coca-Cola turn
Santa Claus red?

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:49.210
Well not really, the red and white coat dates
back well before Coke's 30 year long Christmas

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.410
advertising campaign.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:57.620
It's believed the red originates from St.
Nicholas, who was the Bishop of Myra, Red

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.630
and white were the hues of traditional bishop
robes.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:08.060
The truth is Santa has been portrayed wearing
various coloured robes including both red

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.780
and green, for centuries, in children's literature
and paintings.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:20.760
However Coke are largely responsible for popularising
the jolly red image of Santa we know today

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.470
and enforcing it as the status quo all around
the globe.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:31.090
But getting in touch with Santa is easier
than you may think, he has a real zip code.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:36.360
Every year postal services all over the world
are flooded with letters to Santa.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:42.440
The Canadian Post Office received so many
they set up a special zip code for Santa,

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.520
it's H0H 0H0.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.900
You can write a letter addressed to that zip
code in any language and you will receive

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.470
a letter back [image
] from Santa.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.890
But where does the tradition of Christmas
trees come from?

00:07:55.890 --> 00:08:02.050
The evergreen fir tree has been used to celebrate
both Pagan and Christian winter festivals

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.090
for thousands of years.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:08.470
Nobody is really sure when they were first
used as Christmas trees.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:13.400
But it probably started about 1,000 years
ago in northern Europe.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:20.120
However many early Christmas trees were hung
upside down from the ceiling using chains.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:27.060
The first documented use of a tree to celebrate
Christmas was in the town square of Riga,

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.400
the capital of Latvia in the year 1510.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.870
The money behind the world's biggest holiday
is astronomical.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:42.450
Americans spend about $469 billion on Christmas
shopping each year.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:51.460
The average American spends $707 on gifts,
but surprisingly they're number 2 on the biggest

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.940
spenders list.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:58.860
The tiny European country of Luxembourg are
the world's most generous Christmas spenders,

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:03.890
with each Luxembourgian spending $800 on gifts.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:08.330
And guess what, Santa Claus is the world's
richest man.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:13.750
According to Forbes list of the 15 richest
fictional people.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:20.560
Santa Claus tops the list with a net worth
of infinity.

